# Copy this file to `Makefile.local` (not tracked by git -- see .gitignore) to
# override build settings for your own machine without touching the shared
# Makefile or affecting other contributors.
#
# Most people on a reasonably current GPU don't need this file at all -- the
# default `gpu_target ?= ccnative` in the main Makefile auto-detects your GPU.
# This is only needed if:
#   - Your GPU's architecture has been dropped from your HPC SDK's *bundled*
#     CUDA toolkit (this happened to Maxwell/Pascal/Volta as of CUDA 13.x),
#     requiring an explicit -gpu=ccXY plus an external, older CUDA toolkit.
#   - You want to pin a specific architecture for some other reason (e.g.
#     cross-compiling for a GPU that isn't in the build machine).
#
# Worked example below: a Quadro P4000 (Pascal, compute capability 6.1) paired
# with an HPC SDK install whose bundled CUDA toolkit (13.2) no longer
# generates code for Pascal. The fix is a standalone, external CUDA 12.x
# toolkit (installed separately, NOT part of the HPC SDK -- see
# https://developer.nvidia.com/cuda-toolkit-archive), pointed to via
# NVHPC_CUDA_HOME. Check your own GPU's compute capability with `nvaccelinfo`,
# and adjust ccXY and the CUDA toolkit version to match your driver's reported
# compatibility (also shown by `nvaccelinfo`).

# gpu_target := cc61
# export NVHPC_CUDA_HOME := /usr/local/cuda-12.2
